chmod, change mode, the command for setting file/directory permissions. 2 common ways to use it: octal (numbers) and symbols (letters).
  1. chmod 555 testShell00 ----------- octal notation
      read (r) = 4
      write (w) = 2
      execute (x) = 1
      add them up for each of the three categories (owner, group, others):
        5 = 4+1 = read + execute (no write) ------ r-x
      so if we use 555, it assigns -r-xr-xr-x for all 3 categories
  2. chmod u-x testShell00 --------------- symbolic notation
      structured as: who + operations + permission
      who: u (user/owner), g(group), o(others), a(all)
      operation: +(add), -(remove), =(set exactly)
      permission: r, w, x
      so u-x means: for user(owner), remove the execute permission.
      so from -r-xr-xr-x, we will get -r--r-xr-x
  But yes, there are more ways to reach the same results that we want.
  For example:
    chmod g+w testShell00     # add write permission for group
    chmod o-r testShell00     # remove read permission for others
    chmod a+x testShell00     # add execute for everyone (user, group, others)
    chmod u=rwx,g=rx,o=rx testShell00   # set exact permissions for each category in one command

