l: it's a symbolic link, not a regular file or directory. A symlink is like a shortcut/pointer to another file. Create it with ln -s (link, symbolic)
ln -s test0 test6  
  this creates test6 as a symlink pointing to test0 ------------ matching test6 -> test0
ln: link, create a link between files. There are 2 types:
  1. hard link. ln without -s, creates another name that points directly to the same underlying data on disk. Both names are equally "real"; there's no concept of one being the "original" and one being a "copy."
  2. symbolic link / symlink. ln -s, This creates a special small file whose only content is a path pointing to another file or directory. Think of it like a shortcut on Windows, or an alias on Mac.
