The task asks me to do the following:
  %> tar -cf exo2.tar *
So why there is a * here:
  * is a wildcard (also called a "glob pattern") that the shell expands before the command even runs.
The shell itself looks at your current directory and replaces * with all the files and folders in the current directory (except hidden ones starting with a dot). 
So if your ex02 folder contains test0 through test6, the shell actually turns your command into:
  tar -cf exo2.tar test0 test1 test2 test3 test4 test5 test6
* is a wildcard that matches all files and directories in the current location (except hidden ones — anything starting with a ., like .git or .bashrc, is excluded by default).

A couple of related wildcards worth knowing, since they follow the same idea:

* → matches any number of characters (matches everything)
test* → matches anything starting with "test" (e.g. test0, test1, testXYZ)
*.txt → matches anything ending in .txt
? → matches exactly one character (e.g. test? matches test0, test1, but not test10)

So in your case, since ex02 should only contain test0 through test6, running ls first to confirm exactly that — nothing more, nothing less — before you run tar -cf exo2.tar * is a good habit, since * will happily scoop up anything else sitting there too.



